    // The band edges are returned by value from a constexpr function instead of being stored in a
    // namespace-scope variable, because no storage class for such a variable works on both GPU backends.
    // Both of the obvious spellings have already been tried and both fail, in opposite directions:
    //   - AMREX_GPU_MANAGED (a real __managed__ object, the way aion/zion below are declared) breaks under
    //     HIP at runtime. Device reads go through a compiler-generated shadow pointer that
    //     __hipRegisterManagedVar fills in at module load, but with -fgpu-rdc this header's
    //     anonymous-namespace copy is emitted once per translation unit and the copy the linked device code
    //     reads is not the one the runtime initialises. The shadow pointer stays null and the first device
    //     read -- get_ionizing_band_photon_energy() in actual_rhs.H -- dies with
    //     "Memory access fault by GPU node-N ... on address (nil)". aion/zion are also odr-used from host
    //     code, which is why they get emitted and registered and these band edges did not.
    //   - A plain constexpr variable breaks under CUDA at compile time. nvcc only folds a const/constexpr
    //     host variable into device code when it has scalar built-in type, so reading a GpuArray one gives
    //     'identifier "ChemBandsHeader_" is undefined in device code'.
    // A constexpr function has no static-storage object at all: every use materialises the literals in
    // place, on host and on device, under both backends, so neither rule can bite. Keep it constexpr --
    // the RadSystem_Traits<problem_t>::ChemBands() specialisations that wrap it are constexpr too.
    AMREX_GPU_HOST_DEVICE AMREX_FORCE_INLINE constexpr auto ChemBandsHeader() -> amrex::GpuArray<double, NumChemBands + 1>
    {
        return {{ @CHEM_BANDS@ }};
    }
